@@ -0,0 +1,18 @@
+All checks done — tests pass, vet is clean. Findings:
+
+## Findings
+
+**Should-fix — KB/ADR contradiction (repo ground rule 2, ADR-0006 precedent).**
+`docs/kb/decisions/ADR-0006-nats-message-contract.md:343-344` documents Route resolution as contract semantics: "shortest path by lane length." This diff changes that to free-flow time and updates only the code comments — the KB is now stale and, per AGENTS.md, must be fixed as part of this change. The same addendum sets the precedent this change should follow: an ADR note with a determinism-behavior statement and a durable-binding check. The 2026-07-24 check ("every extant recording predates Route intents") no longer holds — routed recordings now exist (e.g. `data/recordings/chi-loop-od-peak`). Baked recordings replay as data and stay valid, but warm-starts from old keyframes (ADR-0029) and scenario re-runs with routed vehicles will diverge from the old kernel; that belongs in an ADR-0006 addendum or a small new ADR, in or alongside this commit (docs commits are ungated). The chi-loop-urban measurement cited in the comment is exactly the ADR's motivation section — it's already written, just in the wrong place.
+
+**Nit — godoc attachment** (`engine/routing.go:145-158`). Inserting `freeFlowTime` between `routeTable`'s doc comment and its `func` makes the whole block (including the "routeTable returns…" paragraph) the doc comment of `freeFlowTime`, leaving `routeTable` undocumented. Move `freeFlowTime` (and its two comment lines) above the routeTable comment block.
+
+**Nit — mixed units in the degenerate branch** (`engine/routing.go:152-157`). The `SpeedLimit <= 0` fallback returns meters into a seconds-weighted graph — equivalent to assuming 1 m/s, which penalizes such a lane ~14× versus an urban lane of the same length. Unreachable via `netfile.go:148-150` validation (and the built-in constructors pass a shared `limit`), so it's purely defensive; stating the "≡ 1 m/s" equivalence in the comment would make the arbitrariness explicit.
+
+**Verified clean:**
+- Correctness: reverse-Dijkstra relaxation is right — edge p→u costs `freeFlowTime(u)` (the lane entered), destination lane's own cost correctly excluded, matching the old length convention. The diamond fixture genuinely discriminates (b arm 20 s vs c arm ~12 s under time; 100 m vs 400 m under length) and the test fails against the old weight.
+- Determinism (ADR-0005): IEEE division is exact and cross-platform stable; heap ties still break on lane index; strict `<` relaxation preserves first-fixed-order choice; memoization unchanged; no map iteration. Time weights produce fewer exact ties than length weights, not more.
+- No other consumer depends on the weight convention: `dist[]` is discarded, only next-hop indices escape; lateral depth (ADR-0021) counts lane changes, not weights; ADR-0032 lookahead consumes next hops only. ADR-0034's excess-distance analysis is offline, not this table.
+- `go vet` clean; new + existing routing tests pass (`ok traffic-sim/engine`).
+
+REVIEW-COMPLETE
