+Context gathered — the escape doctrine, `exitWalk`'s walk semantics, `HeldTurn` consumption (`engine/engine.go:547-556`, `engine/engine.go:854`), and `downstreamHold`. Findings:
+
+## Findings
+
+**Should-fix — the entry arm violates the doctrine this diff claims it already follows.**
+`engine/gridlock.go:174-175` (entry arm) still calls `boxBlocked` → `exitBlocked`, which discards `holdSeal`. In `exitWalk`, a downstream red caps room (`engine/rightofway.go:329-331`) and falls through to `return true, holdSeal` — so for the *entry* arm a hold-capped seal reads simply as `blocked=true` and **does** trigger the strand. Take test 3's exact geometry (`TestInBoxDiscriminatorReadsTheSeal`, red-capped empty stubs) and put the stuck vehicle one lane upstream on `nA_0` at the `iJ_0` stop line: `next.Internal` → `boxBlocked` → hold-capped `free < need` → `true` → strand after 300 s of red. The diff's comment (`engine/gridlock.go:186-189`) and the ADR amendment claim "the entry arm already lived by 'a red light is never a trigger'" — that's only true of a red at v's *own* stop line (sigGate holds, boxBlocked false); a red one stub past the box reaches the entry arm through this same holdSeal path, and the diff leaves the two arms asymmetric on exactly the case it refined. Pre-existing behavior, but the diff codifies the opposite claim in comments and ADR; either make the entry arm `blocked && !holdSeal` too, or correct the doctrine text and note the asymmetry deliberately.
+
+**Question — first-hop `HeldTurn` divergence between the discriminator's two picks.**
+`engine/gridlock.go:173` picks `next` with the real `v` (honoring `v.HeldTurn`), but the in-box arm's `exitWalk(v, lane, false)` zeroes `probe.HeldTurn` *before* the first pick from `cur = lane` (`engine/rightofway.go:283-289`). For the original entry use that's right (`next` already consumed the turn), but for the new in-box call the first hop *is* the crossing that will consume the turn — `leaderAt` spends the turn on the first hop for precisely this reason (`engine/engine.go:547-556`). An in-box head with `HeldTurn ≠ 0` on an internal lane with >1 successor would be classified against one exit road while the walk evaluates a different chain. Moot if internal lanes are always single-successor (netimport's movement-per-internal-lane convention suggests so; controllers can set `HeldTurn` any time via intents, `engine/intent.go:175`) — if that invariant is relied on, it's worth a line saying so.
+
+**Nit —** `engine/gridlock.go:177-192`: the ten-line in-box comment precedes the `if !lane.Internal { return false }` guard that establishes the case it describes; guard first would read cleaner.
+
+No blockers. Determinism is clean: `exitWalk` walks slices only, no map iteration on the new path, no clock/RNG; `mergeThreat`/`resetStuckBehind` maps are membership-only. No NATS subject or payload changes (`Stats.Stranded`/`StrandedBySection` pre-exist), so no contract note needed. ADR-0034 amendment is present and matches the code. Tests exercise all three discriminator readings, including the negative (draining) and hold-capped cases; the fixture geometry checks out (tail at S=0.5 with 4.5 m length genuinely seals the 30 m `nE_0` even after wall-clamped compaction).
+
+REVIEW-COMPLETE
