+Vet is clean and the new tests pass. Findings:
+
+## Blocker
+
+**B1. `rerouteTable`'s reachability walk does not actually install the candidate it is checking — a chain that revisits lane `i` escapes through the still-static `mixed[i]`, passes the check, and the subsequent install closes a real routing cycle.** `engine/routing.go:323-337`.
+
+The comment (routing.go:293, and the ADR §2 "with all earlier candidates already installed") specifies the walk simulates the table *with the candidate installed*. The code walks `j = fresh[i]; j = mixed[j]; ...` while `mixed[i]` still holds the static hop. Trace: suppose the mixed chain from `fresh[i]` leads back to `i` (possible in any cyclic network, which chi-loop-urban is — earlier-installed candidates or static hops can point at `i`). The walk reaches `i`, follows `mixed[i] = next[i]` (static), reaches the destination, sets `reached = true`; then `mixed[i] = fresh[i]` creates the cycle `i → fresh[i] → … → i`. The served table now strands every vehicle routed through it in a loop — and if the loop is free-flowing, the floored dwell samples (routing.go's own comment at :274-275) keep its weights at free flow, so no later epoch ever breaks it. This is precisely the pathology the constructive check exists to prevent, and it silently voids the ADR's "never abandon the destination" consequence and the induction argument at routing.go:279-281. The diamond/dual-exit test topologies are acyclic, so no current test can catch it.
+
+Fix is two lines: install `mixed[i] = fresh[i]` before the walk, start the walk at `i` (or keep starting at `fresh[i]`), and revert on `!reached`. With the candidate installed, the walk is a pure function iteration, so any revisit of any node (including `i`) loops until the `hops <= len(mixed)` bound and is correctly rejected — and the induction (installing at `i` only rewrites chains that pass through `i`, all of which now follow `i`'s verified chain) becomes sound. Deterministic either way; this is a correctness bug, not a replay bug. A cyclic-topology test (e.g., a two-lane loop with an exit) should pin it.
+
+## Should-fix
+
+**S1. Stale comments describe the rejected recompute budget/meter as if it exists.** `engine/engine.go:717-718` (Step phase list: "recompute budget reset"), `engine/engine.go:726` ("reset the per-tick recompute budget"), `engine/keyframe.go` ("The next-hop tables and the recompute budget are NOT here"), `engine/adaptive_test.go:216` ("the recompute meter needs two tables") and `:366` ("budget"). The budget was designed and rejected (ADR §2, routing.go:1073-1077 says "unmetered"); these leftovers contradict the shipped design in the two files future readers will trust most — the Step phase contract and the keyframe format doc. Comment-only fix.
+
+## Nit
+
+**N1.** `engine/engine.go:787` — `noteLaneLeave` sets `v.laneEntryTick = e.Tick` even on despawn/strand paths where the vehicle is being removed; harmless but a one-line `if v.Lane != nil`-style guard or comment would spare the next reader the "is this read again?" check. (It is not.)
+
+## Question
+
+**Q1.** `engine/routing.go:319` — the margin test compares `dist[i]` (the *fully fresh* path cost) against the static path, but installs only the single hop; downstream lanes whose own margins didn't clear keep static hops, so the realized mixed-path cost can exceed `dist[i]` by up to the accumulated downstream margins. Bounded and deterministic, so fine for v1 — but worth one sentence in the `rerouteTable` comment so the validation runs' oscillation numbers aren't misread against an idealized cost.
+
+Everything else checked out: flag-off byte-identity (version selection untouched, v6 sections gated on the flag), writer/reader section ordering symmetric, `nLanes` guard, pre-v6-into-flag-on dwell-clock semantics, v6-into-flag-off loud rejection, no map iteration in sampled paths (`routeEpochs`/`staticTabs`/`routeTabs` are keyed access only), fixed-lane-order EMA relaxation, dwell floor/cap, epoch arithmetic consistent across restore at and off boundaries, `freeFlowTime` zero-limit guard present, and the `Params.AdaptiveRouting`-in-`RunMeta` contract change carries its ADR migration note.
+
+REVIEW-COMPLETE
